ust  it's just who i am you know i have this  healthy  way of eating that works for me so i'm  hearing a lot of this kind of this is  who i am rhetoric which tells me that  this routine has become deeply ingrained  in her identity and again not diagnosing  but this is also a core feature of  orthorexia because folks with orthorexia  moralize food as good and bad so when  they do engage in what they perceive to  be a bad eating behavior they often  identify as bad themselves a perfect  example of this is when david recalled a  singular moment in time when he and his  wife shared a meal that victoria  typically would not eat and then she  proceeded to never eat that food ever  again ugh i am so sad to hear something  like that you know often folks who  struggle with food describe intense  long-term fear and guilt after a  singular experience with a forbidden  food and this fear might become even  more ingrained when someone draws  attention to the slip up as david did in  this case even if it was in a positive  light for him this is why with picky  kids for example we actually don't want  to praise them for eating a scary food  we want to remain as neutral as possible  and the same can be said for adults  struggling with food i know how scary it  is to change an aspect of your life when  you believe it is just synonymous with  who you are but no life is inherently so  small there is so much room in this  world to grow and thrive when we let go  of self-imposed rigid restraints so much  attention on eating fat-free and now  what we know is the complete opposite  actually i mean my diet now consists of  a lot of healthy fats i mean  numerous avocados for example and seeds  and nuts  yes the fat-free craze of the 90s i do  not miss it and the collective  acceptance of fats today just goes to  show why we really should never be  married to any of these changing food  fats having said that i am very relieved  to hear that victoria is insisting on  you know more than just f